15799:### Task 3 — mod install: a real, generic mechanism already exists for 12 families; Terraria has two independent, genuine blockers
15801:> **Superseded 2026-09-30** — both blockers below are fixed on branch `feature/terraria-real-mod-install`
15803:> `/tshock/ServerPlugins`" was the wrong fix. See "2026-09-30 — One-click TShock plugins for Terraria" at
15808:**Terraria is not wired, for two independent, genuine, and confirmed reasons — not because nobody got to it:**
15810:1. **The seeded catalog is the wrong platform for what Nxeon actually runs.** All 1000 seeded `GameMod` rows for `terraria` are `source: "steam_workshop"` — real content, but it's tModLoader's Workshop catalogue (tModLoader ships as its own separate Steam app; 1.4+ tModLoader mods are distributed through Steam Workshop under tModLoader's own listing, not Terraria's). Nxeon's actual Terraria product (`prisma/catalog.ts`'s `terraria` OsTemplate) runs `ryshe/terraria` — **vanilla Terraria + TShock, tModLoader is never installed.** There is no process on the server that would ever read a Workshop mod id or load a `.tmod` file. Auto-downloading one of these 1000 items would produce an inert file sitting unused — a fake "installed" state. A real fix means offering a second, tModLoader-based Terraria server variant; that's a product decision, not a mechanical wiring job, and out of scope here.
15811:2. **Even for the content type Nxeon's real Terraria server CAN use** — TShock plugins, genuine `.dll` files typically distributed as GitHub release assets (which the already-built `"github"` `RESOLVABLE_SOURCES` entry can fetch) — **there is nowhere to put one.** TShock installs at `/tshock` inside the `ryshe/terraria` container (confirmed via the image's own `/tshock/bootstrap.sh`, already cited in `prisma/catalog.ts`'s own comment for an earlier fix), and the template's only bind mount is `-v /opt/terraria:/root/.local/share/Terraria/Worlds` — the world-save folder. `/tshock/ServerPlugins` is not reachable from the host at all today. This means **neither an automated installer nor a customer's own manual "upload it in Files" can actually reach TShock's real plugin folder right now** — the honest fallback message is directionally correct but slightly understates the gap (it isn't just "not automated yet," it's "not reachable yet, automated or not").
15813:A real fix for reason 2 (bind-mount `/tshock/ServerPlugins` to a new host path in `prisma/catalog.ts`'s setupScript, add a `terraria` case to `worker/game-mods.ts`'s `modDirShell`, add `"terraria"` to `INSTALLABLE_FAMILIES`, and reseed the catalog with real TShock plugins instead of tModLoader Workshop items) is genuinely buildable and was fully scoped out — but was **not shipped this pass**, because a Docker bind mount over a path the image populates at build time can silently blank out whatever the image put there, and that must be verified against a real running container before it ships to a real product's provisioning script; it is not safe to ship blind. As documented under Bug 2 above, this session hit an explicit, repeated platform/permission boundary against provisioning new real test hardware (staging's sandbox flag, and a production workaround), so that live verification could not be done. Shipping an unverified infrastructure change to `prisma/catalog.ts` risks a real regression for every future Terraria order — worse than leaving today's honest gate in place. **Documented the full reasoning directly in `lib/mod-install-support.ts` (a large, permanent code comment) so the next person doesn't have to re-derive any of this, and left the actual mechanism exactly as-is: no `INSTALLABLE_FAMILIES` entry, no catalog change.** A follow-up with the full technical plan for whoever can safely provision test infrastructure is flagged separately.
